<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
	The Watch target used to generate this file from INFOPLIST_KEY_* build
	settings. It is authored now because the runtime claim a Watch recording
	needs — WKBackgroundModes — is an array, and an array is awkward to express
	as a build setting. See docs/milestones/milestone-1.6.md, spike A.

	CFBundleIcons and NSAccentColorName are deliberately absent: the asset
	catalog compiler injects both from ASSETCATALOG_COMPILER_APPICON_NAME and
	ASSETCATALOG_COMPILER_GLOBAL_ACCENT_COLOR_NAME. That injection is what lets
	the Champagne accent reach the Watch with no Swift change at all (see
	docs/architecture/visual-identity.md), so if the accent ever disappears from
	the Watch, check the built Info.plist for NSAccentColorName before touching
	any code.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>CFBundleDevelopmentRegion</key>
	<string>$(DEVELOPMENT_LANGUAGE)</string>
	<key>CFBundleDisplayName</key>
	<string>Onbii</string>
	<key>CFBundleExecutable</key>
	<string>$(EXECUTABLE_NAME)</string>
	<key>CFBundleIdentifier</key>
	<string>$(PRODUCT_BUNDLE_IDENTIFIER)</string>
	<key>CFBundleInfoDictionaryVersion</key>
	<string>6.0</string>
	<key>CFBundleName</key>
	<string>$(PRODUCT_NAME)</string>
	<key>CFBundlePackageType</key>
	<string>APPL</string>
	<key>CFBundleShortVersionString</key>
	<string>$(MARKETING_VERSION)</string>
	<key>CFBundleVersion</key>
	<string>$(CURRENT_PROJECT_VERSION)</string>
	<key>NSLocationWhenInUseUsageDescription</key>
	<string>Onbii records where a recording was captured, so you can remember where a conversation happened. Location is optional and travels with the recording to your iPhone.</string>
	<key>NSMicrophoneUsageDescription</key>
	<string>Onbii records audio only after you tap Record, then transfers the original recording to your iPhone.</string>
	<!--
		WKBackgroundModes is deliberately absent, and that appears to be fine.

		Measured on watchOS 27, 27 July 2026: with no background mode declared at
		all, leaving the app leaves the microphone live and recording, with the
		system's own indicator showing. Siri and a timer did not disturb it.

		This corrects the first field test's reasoning, which assumed that
		without a declared mode watchOS "was entitled to suspend it". Entitled
		perhaps; it does not appear to exercise that. The recording that morning
		did stop after 1200 s, and the likelier cause is that the Workout app
		took over — which is a specific collision to solve, not a missing claim
		on runtime. See docs/field-tests/2026-07-27-field-test-1.md.

		So do not add a mode speculatively. Two that were considered are
		rejected on the merits rather than untested: `mindfulness` and
		`self-care` back WKExtendedRuntimeSession, which describes a bounded
		stationary practice — a reflection walk is not one, and stretching the
		definition to fit would be a claim this project should not make.

		`audio` remains the only plausible addition, and only if a recording is
		found to die in the background for want of it. Giving the Watch a longer
		or stronger claim on runtime is a *feature* — an HKWorkoutSession for a
		walk that is genuinely happening, saved as a workout — and belongs in
		docs/architecture/watch-capture-modes.md, not here.
	-->
	<key>WKApplication</key>
	<true/>
	<key>WKCompanionAppBundleIdentifier</key>
	<string>com.yepyr.onbii</string>
	<key>WKRunsIndependentlyOfCompanionApp</key>
	<false/>
</dict>
</plist>
